# gtm-crawler

Evidence-first company intelligence crawler. Given a company website, it
collects public evidence for a structured GTM company dossier (company,
product, traction and competitor fields).

Every value in the dossier must trace back to a quoted source on a fetched
page. Fields without sufficient evidence are reported as `UNKNOWN`; conflicting
sources are preserved rather than resolved by picking one.

> Status: V1 pipeline implemented end to end: crawling (robots.txt, sitemaps,
> prioritized durable frontier, guarded fetching, browser fallback), a
> bounded external phase (reference profiles, Exa search), rule-based
> extraction of company, product, traction and competitor evidence (no LLM),
> field assessment with corroboration and conflicts, and a cited dossier;
> plus the production service layer (job API, leased workers that survive
> crashes, Prometheus metrics, S3-compatible raw store). Run against a few
> live company sites in development (small, bounded crawls); acceptance and
> load evidence is against a simulated web (docs/production-readiness.md).

## Requirements

- Python 3.11+
- Docker (for the local PostgreSQL 16 instance)

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv .venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate     Linux/macOS: source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"
playwright install chromium        # browser fallback (or set browser.enabled = false)

docker compose up -d --wait        # PostgreSQL on 127.0.0.1:5432
alembic upgrade head               # create the schema
```

## Running a crawl

Set `identity.user_agent_contact` first (the crawler refuses to run without
it), then:

```bash
gtm-crawl https://example.com            # or: python -m gtm_crawler.cli ...
```

The command crawls the company's site, extracts and assesses it, searches
only for what the site left open (the bounded external phase), then resolves
and crawls its competitors, and prints the crawl ids, statuses, statistics and
extraction counts together with the `dossier` (see below) as JSON. Exit code 0
for `completed`/`partial`, 1 for `failed`, 2 for configuration or URL errors.

After improving extraction rules, re-run them over a stored crawl without
fetching anything (no user-agent contact needed):

```bash
gtm-crawl --reextract 42                 # crawl id from an earlier run
```

This is a new extraction run followed by a fresh assessment and dossier. The
crawl's earlier runs stay in the database as history but no longer count.
To see what changed first, use `--replay` (see Replay and regression).

Apply the raw-data retention policy (see Raw document store) with
`gtm-crawl --prune`, e.g. from a daily scheduled job.

## Running as a service

For production the pipeline runs as jobs: `gtm-api` records requests and
serves results, and any number of `gtm-worker` processes run the jobs.

```bash
gtm-api                                  # 127.0.0.1:8000 (api.host / api.port)
gtm-worker                               # repeat on more machines to scale out
curl -X POST localhost:8000/v1/crawls -H 'content-type: application/json' \
     -d '{"root_url": "https://example.com"}'
```

| Endpoint | |
|---|---|
| `POST /v1/crawls` | `{"root_url", "domain"?, "idempotency_key"?}` (or an `Idempotency-Key` header). 201 with a new job; 200 with the existing one for the same key, or the company's unfinished job; 409 if the key was already used for another `root_url` |
| `GET /v1/crawls/{id}` | Status (`CREATED`, `QUEUED`, `RUNNING`, `FINALIZING`, `COMPLETED`, `PAUSED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELLED`), timestamps, error, versions, the job's site crawls, result summary |
| `POST /v1/crawls/{id}/pause`, `/resume`, `/cancel` | Pause or cancel (at once if queued, else at the next safe point) / put a paused job back in the queue |
| `GET /v1/crawls/{id}/quality` | The job's crawl-quality report (see [Metrics](#metrics)) |
| `GET /v1/companies/{id}/dossier` | The current dossier, built on request; `?clean=true` for plain values only (`dossier.clean_dossier`) |
| `GET /v1/companies/{id}/cost` | What crawling the company cost: total, by kind (cost and usage by unit), and per job (see Cost accounting) |
| `GET /v1/dead-letters` (`?include_resolved=true`, `limit`) | The dead-letter queue: open items with entity, URL or job, error class, error, attempts |
| `POST /v1/dead-letters/{id}/requeue`, `/discard` | Put the work back (a URL into its crawl's frontier with fresh attempts, if the crawl is still running or paused; a job as a new job), or close the item; 409 if already resolved |
| `GET /livez`, `GET /readyz`, `GET /metrics` | Liveness (process answers); readiness (database reachable and schema not behind this code's migration head, else 503; a newer release's schema answers 200 with `schema_ahead`); Prometheus metrics. `/healthz` = `/readyz` |

A "crawl" in the API is a job: the company's site, the external phase and its
competitors' sites. With `api.token` set (use `GTM__API__TOKEN`), `/v1`
requests need `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; while rotating, the old token in
`api.previous_token` is accepted too.

How the pieces stay correct when things fail:

- **Leases.** A worker claims a job with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` and renews
  its lease by heartbeat (`worker.lease_seconds`); URLs inside a crawl are
  claimed the same way. If a worker dies, its leases expire and another
  worker resumes the job from the database: the queue, per-URL states and a
  periodic checkpoint of the crawl's counters. Pages already fetched are not
  fetched again.
- **Pause** and **cancel** are delivered by the heartbeat; the crawl stops
  claiming URLs, finishes those in flight and saves its state. A budget stop
  is `COMPLETED`; the result keeps the stop reason.
- **History.** Every job transition is checked against the allowed moves
  (`gtm_crawler/domain.py`) and appended to `crawl_event` with who made it
  and why; that table cannot be updated or deleted.
- **Shutdown** (SIGTERM, Ctrl+C) hands the job back at once for another
  worker. A database outage leaves the job unacknowledged; it resumes when
  the database is back. A job claimed `max_job_attempts` times without
  finishing is failed, so a job that crashes workers cannot loop forever.
- **Retry policy by error class** (`fetch.http.retry_class`): 429 / 503
  wait the server's `Retry-After` (else `retry_delay_rate_limited_seconds`),
  DNS failures `retry_delay_dns_seconds`, other transient failures
  (timeouts, connection errors, 5xx) `retry_delay_seconds`; everything else
  (404, TLS, too large, blocked) is not retried.
- **Dead-letter queue** (`dead_letter_item`, `gtm_crawler/deadletter.py`):
  a URL still failing after `max_url_attempts`, a URL whose lease ran out
  `max_url_lease_expiries` times (it may be what kills the worker), a URL
  whose processing raised an unexpected error (the crawl goes on without
  it), a job abandoned after `max_job_attempts`, and a job failed by an
  unexpected error. The work moves to `dlq` (URLs) or `FAILED` (jobs) in the
  same transaction that writes the item; at most one item is open per URL of
  a crawl and per job, so a repeated write is harmless. Inspect, requeue or
  discard through the API.
- **Backpressure.** New jobs are refused with 429 and `Retry-After` while
  `api.max_queued_jobs` are waiting for a worker. Inside a crawl each URL is
  one task through fetch, store, parse and database writes, and at most
  `crawl.concurrency` run at once, so a slow stage slows claiming instead of
  building a backlog. Queue depth is exported at scrape time:
  `queue_jobs{state}`, `queue_urls{state}` (running crawls),
  `dead_letter_open{entity}`, and `dead_letter_items_total`.
- **Resuming** refuses changed `urls`, `parse` or `frontier` settings: pages
  and evidence locators already stored depend on them.

### Deployment

`docker compose --profile app up -d --build` runs PostgreSQL, the migrations,
the API (published on 127.0.0.1:8000) and a worker from one image (see
`Dockerfile`; Chromium included, runs as a non-root user). Scale workers with
`--scale worker=3`. Secrets come from the environment or an uncommitted
`.env`: `GTM_CONTACT` (required user-agent contact), `EXA_API_KEY`,
`GTM_API_TOKEN`. `docker compose --profile s3 up -d s3` starts a local
S3-compatible store (SeaweedFS, unauthenticated, development only) on
127.0.0.1:8333 for trying the S3 raw store.

Size the database pool for the worker's concurrency (the worker uses
`crawl.concurrency + 2` connections plus overflow), back up PostgreSQL and
the raw store together, and scale workers only once measurements show the
need.

### Staging and rollout

`GTM_ENV=staging` fails closed exactly like `prod`. `deploy/config.staging.toml`
and `deploy/config.prod.toml` (identical; no secrets) hold conservative initial
budgets. After every deploy run the smoke test, which checks probes, auth,
idempotent submission, a full crawl, dossier/quality/cost and that every
non-UNKNOWN field's lineage ends in a stored raw artifact:

```bash
GTM_SMOKE_TOKEN=... python -m gtm_crawler.smoke --api https://gtm.staging.internal     --site https://www.example.com/
```

Extraction changes pass `deploy/canary-gate.sh <prod-ref>` first (additive
migrations only, corpus, replay of a restored production copy with
`--fail-on-change`), then workers roll out gradually. `/readyz` stays ready on
a schema a newer release has migrated. Rotate the API token without downtime
with `GTM__API__PREVIOUS_TOKEN`. `deploy/staging-test/run.sh` rehearses all of
this on a local compose stack (test-only). Details, rollback and the migration
downgrade policy: `docs/staging.md`.

### Production readiness

`python -m gtm_crawler.acceptance [--full] [--out report.json]` demonstrates
each acceptance criterion of spec section 35 by running the tests and tools
that prove it (one pytest run over the chosen node ids, the corpus, a seeded
`--replay-all --fail-on-change`, the restore drill and every load scenario;
`--full` uses the load scenarios' full scale and a 1000-page drill) and prints
a matrix of criterion, evidence, PASS / FAIL / NOT DEMONSTRATED and duration;
exit 1 unless every criterion passes. A skipped test (no Chromium, no
`pg_dump`) is NOT DEMONSTRATED, never PASS. Scratch databases:
`--load-database-url`, `--drill-database-url`, `--replay-database-url` (names
must contain "load", "drill", "acceptance"; they are dropped and recreated);
tests use `--test-database-url` / `GTM_TEST_DATABASE_URL`. Measured results,
what "target scale" covers, the go-live checklist, the first-week ramp plan
and open decisions: [docs/production-readiness.md](docs/production-readiness.md).

## Configuration

Settings are read from `config.toml` in the working directory (or the file
named by `$GTM_CONFIG`); see `config.example.toml`. Any key can be overridden
with an environment variable `GTM__<SECTION>__<KEY>`, for example
`GTM__DATABASE__URL`. Unknown keys are rejected so typos cannot silently fall
back to defaults.

- **Profiles.** `GTM_ENV=dev|test|staging|prod` also loads `config.<env>.toml`
  next to the base file (its values win; environment variables win over both).
  `staging` and `prod` fail closed: it refuses to start without `identity.user_agent_contact`
  and `api.token`, with the development database URL, without a statement
  timeout, or with DEBUG logging.
- **Secrets.** `database.url`, `search.api_key` and `api.token` are secrets:
  shown as `***` in `repr`, redacted from logs, never stored in crawl
  configuration snapshots. Set them through the environment.
- **Feature flags.** `[features] enabled = [...]` turns on experimental logic;
  only flags the code declares are accepted.
- **Database.** `[database]` sets the pool (`pool_size`, `max_overflow`,
  `pool_timeout`), `connect_timeout_seconds`, and server-side
  `statement_timeout_ms` / `lock_timeout_ms` for every connection.
- **Versions.** Each crawl and job records the versions of the crawler,
  parser, extractors, assessment method, dossier schema, search templates
  and a hash of the output-affecting configuration (`gtm_crawler/versions.py`;
  the environment name, raw-store location, logging, prices and contact
  address are left out, so staging and prod with equal budgets hash alike).

## Database

The schema separates what sources say from what the system concludes:

| Table | Holds |
|---|---|
| `organization` | Crawl targets, competitors, investors, partners |
| `crawl_job` | Requested pipeline runs: status, lease, pause/cancel request, attempts, versions, result summary |
| `crawl_event` | Append-only history of job state changes: from, to, actor, reason |
| `crawl`, `crawl_url` | Site crawls (with config snapshot and resume checkpoint) and the durable frontier: each URL's state, lease, attempts and history |
| `robots_snapshot` | Each robots.txt load: origin, status, HTTP code, raw body hash |
| `sitemap_fetch` | Each sitemap request: status, error, URLs and child sitemaps found, raw body hash |
| `extraction` | Extraction runs over a crawl; the latest run's observations are current |
| `url`, `fetch`, `page`, `link` | Normalized URLs, every retrieval attempt, parsed pages, outbound links |
| `evidence` | Exact quote plus its location (XPath / JSON path, character offsets) |
| `observation` | One value stated by one source, verbatim and (when exact) normalized with its qualifier |
| `relationship` | Sourced links between organizations (investor, partner, competitor) |
| `product` | Products identified for an organization |
| `field_assessment` | Versioned conclusion per field: state, value, confidence, supporting observations |

`evidence`, `observation`, `relationship` and `field_assessment` are
append-only (enforced by database triggers). Check constraints stop a
`field_assessment` from carrying a value unless it cites observations, and
stop `UNKNOWN`, `CONFLICTING` and `NOT_APPLICABLE` fields from carrying a
value at all.

Raw HTML is not stored in the database; `fetch.raw_sha256` references the
content-addressed raw store.

Schema changes go through Alembic migrations in `gtm_crawler/db/migrations`.

## Evidence and provenance

Extractors never write observations directly. They build an
`ObservationDraft` with a `Quote` (from `gtm_crawler.evidence`: `find_quote` /
`quote_block` for text, located by the block's XPath plus character offsets;
`quote_json` for a JSON-LD scalar, located by JSON path; `quote_meta` for a
`<meta>` tag's content, located as `key[index]`) and pass it to
`gtm_crawler.db.evidence.EvidenceStore.record`, which re-parses the page's raw
document and refuses, storing nothing from the batch, when:

- the quote is not exactly at its claimed location on that page;
- a `stated` value is not word-for-word in its quote, or cuts off a qualifier
  (`500 customers` out of `more than 500 customers`, `500` out of `500+`);
- a normalized value has no explicit qualifier (`eq`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`,
  `lte`, `approx`, `range`), so a claim like "more than 500" is stored as
  `{"qualifier": "gt", "value": 500}`, never as a bare 500.

`derived` observations (e.g. a normalized headquarters location) still cite
the quote they were derived from. Source tier, source type, first-party flag,
publication date and retrieval time are filled in from the page and its fetch.

Source hierarchy (`classify_source`, relative to the observation's company):
tier 1 for the company's own site (typed as pricing, documentation, press
release, investor information, product page or general website) and for
regulators and registries; tier 2 for business databases and major
publications; tier 3 for industry publications and professional profiles;
tier 4 for any other third party. Domain lists are in `[sources]`. Tier
describes source quality only.

`EvidenceStore.trace(observation_id)` returns the whole chain (value, quote,
locator, page and its parser version, extractor version, URL, retrieval event
with status and time, raw artifact with size, format version and whether
retention pruned it, published / retrieved / observed times, crawl).
`EvidenceStore.field_lineage(organization_id, field)` starts one step
earlier, at the field's latest assessment (every item of a list field), and
traces every observation it cites; a field with no assessment is UNKNOWN with
no sources. The API serves it at `GET /v1/companies/{id}/lineage/{field}`. Dossier
citations carry the evidence id, locator, page, fetch, artifact hash and
parser / extractor versions. `EvidenceStore.verify(evidence_id)` re-parses the raw
document with the crawl's own parse settings and confirms the quote is still
exactly there. `evidence_raw_digests()` is the keep set for `RawStore.prune`.

Observation `confidence` is computed, not supplied by extractors:
`extraction_certainty × source weight × kind factor`. Source weights:
regulator/registry 0.95, the company's own site 0.90, tier 2 0.80, tier 3
0.65, tier 4 0.45; kind factor: stated 1.0, derived 0.9. It scores a single
observation from a single source. Corroboration, conflicts, freshness and
the thresholds for `CORROBORATED` / `SUPPORTED` are assessed per field
(`field_assessment`), so no single first-party statement can make a field
corroborated on its own.

## Extraction

After a crawl, `gtm-crawl` runs the rule-based extractors
(`gtm_crawler.extract.extract_crawl`; no LLM) over every non-duplicate page,
re-parsed from its raw document, and records observations through
`EvidenceStore`. A statement repeated across one site is recorded once per
run; the same statement on another site is kept, because that is
corroboration. Each call is a new `extraction` run: assessment, competitor
resolution and the dossier use only the latest run of each crawl, so
re-extraction replaces rather than duplicates. Drafts that fail their own evidence checks are counted as `rejected`
and logged, never stored.

### Company information

| Field | From |
|---|---|
| `company_name`, `legal_name`, `website`, `company_description` | JSON-LD Organization `name`, `legalName`, `url`, `description`; `og:site_name`; home page meta description |
| `founded_year` | JSON-LD `foundingDate`; "founded / established / incorporated in YYYY" |
| `employee_count`, `company_size` | JSON-LD `numberOfEmployees` (exact value, or `minValue` / `maxValue` as `gte` / `lte`); "more than 250 employees", "up to 250", "fewer than 50", "1,200+ employees", "51-200 employees" (qualifiers kept, the same vocabulary as other counts: `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`/`approx`) |
| `headquarters` | "headquartered in …", "our headquarters is in …", "HQ: …" |
| `company_location`, `company_country` | JSON-LD `address` (a postal address is not treated as the headquarters); "based in …" |
| `ownership_type` | JSON-LD `tickerSymbol`, `parentOrganization`; "publicly traded", "NASDAQ: XYZ", "privately held", "venture-backed", "subsidiary of …", "employee-owned", "non-profit" |
| `business_model` | B2B, B2C, SaaS, marketplace, subscription, open source, freemium, usage-based, only as a description of the company itself ("we are a B2B SaaS platform", not "we help SaaS companies") |
| `market_domain` | JSON-LD `naics` / `isicV4`; "for the logistics industry" |

Rules favour precision. Text is attributed to the company only when its
sentence refers to the company itself ("we", "our", or a name the site uses
for itself in JSON-LD or `og:site_name`), so a blog post about another company
yields nothing. JSON-LD Organization nodes count only at the top level (not a
nested publisher or customer) and only if any `url` they give is on the site.

### Products

A product is identified (a `product` row) only from:

1. a JSON-LD `Product`, `SoftwareApplication`, `WebApplication` or
   `MobileApplication` node the site publishes about itself (not one whose
   `url` is on another domain), or
2. a page at the product's own URL (`/products/<slug>`, `/product/<slug>`,
   `/apps/<slug>`) whose `<h1>` or `og:title` names it.

Feature and pricing pages alone are never products, and products are never
merged because they share a site: two sightings are one product only if their
slugs match (lowercased name with the company's own name dropped from the
front). The number of products is the count of `product` rows.

| Field | From |
|---|---|
| `product_name`, `product_description`, `product_category` | JSON-LD `name`, `description`, `applicationCategory` / `category`; product page `<h1>` / `og:title` and meta description |
| `product_positioning` | the product page's opening paragraph after its `<h1>`, quoted as written |
| `key_feature`, `main_use_case` | JSON-LD `featureList`; sub-headings and list items under a "Features" / "Capabilities" or "Use cases" heading |
| `price`, `plan_name` | pricing pages (`/pricing`, `/plans`, …) and JSON-LD `offers`. Prices keep their wording ("Starting at €99 per user per month") with amount, currency, period and `gte` for "from / starting at" normalized; a billing cycle is its own key ("$249/mo billed annually": `per: ["month"]`, `billing: "annual"`); "Contact sales" is recorded as `contact_sales`, never a number. A price's plan (the heading above it) is its `qualifier`. |
| `recent_update` | changelog / release-note / news / press pages: headings carrying their own date, or the `<h1>` of a page with an explicit publication date. Undated items are not reported as updates. |

Observations from a product's own page or JSON-LD node carry its
`product_id`; company-wide pages (`/features`, `/pricing`, `/changelog`)
produce observations with no product, since which product they describe is
not stated.

### Traction

Traction figures are recorded only as stated. No figure is ever computed from
another: funding is never a valuation, a round is never total funding,
customers are never an exact count unless the source gives one, and nothing
is estimated from prices, traffic or headcount.

| Field | Recognized statements |
|---|---|
| `arr`, `mrr` | "$10M ARR", "annual recurring revenue exceeded $2.5 million", "over $400K MRR" |
| `annual_revenue` | "$30M in annual revenue", "revenue was €30 million" |
| `customer_count` | "more than 500 customers", "2,000+ companies use Acme" |
| `customer_concentration` | "our largest customer accounted for 18% of total revenue", "no single customer represents more than 10% of revenue" (kept verbatim) |
| `funding_round` | "raised a $20M Series A", "closed its seed round of $3M"; a raise with no round named has `round: null` |
| `total_funding` | only when stated as a total: "raised $50M to date", "total funding of $75M", "raised a total of …" |
| `valuation` | "valued at $1.2 billion", "a $500M post-money valuation" |
| `investor` | "led by …", "backed by …", "with participation from …", "investors include …"; JSON-LD `funder` |
| `technology_partnership`, `strategic_partnership` | "technology partners include …", "strategic partnership with …", "an AWS Advanced Technology Partner", names under "Technology partners" / "Strategic partners" headings |
| `partnership` | a partnership whose kind is not stated ("partnered with …"); type `unspecified`, no relationship |
| `product_sales` | "sold more than 10,000 units" (on a product's page, linked to it) |

Amounts keep their wording and are normalized as `{qualifier, amount,
currency}` (K / M / B / million / billion / lakh / crore scaled); a year
stated in the sentence ("in 2025", "FY2024") is added as `year`. Investors
and typed partnerships are also stored as `relationship` rows citing the
observation; organizations known only by name are matched by exact name.

A statement counts only if the company is its subject: a self-reference
("we", "our", or the company's name) must come before the claim with no other
proper noun in between, so "we congratulate our customer Globex on raising
$50M" records nothing. The website
domain and subdomains come from the crawl's own fetch records rather than
from page statements.

### GTM signals

`gtm_crawler/extract/signals.py` (production spec section 17) adds, with the
same subject rules:

| Field | From |
|---|---|
| `customer` | "our customers include A, B and C"; on the company's site, a sentence opening "Trusted by A and B" (a list ending in a generic noun, "Fortune 500 companies", names no one) |
| `case_study` | the `<h1>` of `/customers/<slug>`, `/case-studies/<slug>`, `/customer-stories/<slug>` pages |
| `integration` | "integrates with A, B and C"; the product an `/integrations/<slug>` page's `<h1>` names ("Acme + Salesforce") when that is unambiguous |
| `job_opening` | JSON-LD `JobPosting` titles for the company (date posted as `as_of`) |
| `open_roles` | "we have more than 40 open positions" (qualifier kept) |
| `office_location` | "we have offices in London, New York and Berlin" |
| `website_technology` | the site's `<meta name="generator">`; nothing is inferred from scripts, headers or markup |

Page-level signals count only on the company's own site. Press and news are
the `recent_update` field (dated headings on news, press and changelog pages).

## Field assessment

After extraction, `gtm_crawler.assess.assess_organization` appends one
`field_assessment` row per field for the company, each of its products and
each crawled competitor (method `fa-v2`: the approved `fa-v1` rules with the
spec's field states, VERIFIED → `CORROBORATED`, FOUND → `SUPPORTED`,
UNVERIFIED → `UNSUPPORTED`, plus `STALE`). Rows are never
updated: the latest per field is current, earlier ones are history.

1. **Independence.** Observations from the same site (registrable domain of
   the page) are one source; a repeated statement is not corroboration.
2. **Agreement.** Equal normalized values agree; a bound and a value inside it
   are compatible ("more than 500" and "620" gives 620). Numbers are compared
   as intervals (`approx` = ±10%) and never across currencies; places by word
   containment ("Austin" vs "Austin, Texas"); ownership by fixed incompatible
   pairs (public vs private, …). Anything else conflicts.
3. **Time.** For figures that change (employees, customers, ARR/MRR, revenue,
   funding, valuation, unit sales), a newer dated statement supersedes an
   older one, which stays as history and is not cited; disagreements within
   the latest period or involving undated statements are conflicts.
4. **Confidence** = 1 − ∏(1 − best observation confidence per source), at most
   0.99.
5. **State.** None observed → `UNKNOWN`. Unresolved disagreement →
   `CONFLICTING` with no value and every disagreeing observation cited. Two or
   more independent sources, one of tier 1–2, confidence ≥ 0.80 → `CORROBORATED`.
   Confidence ≥ 0.60 → `SUPPORTED`. Otherwise `UNSUPPORTED`: the claim is
   kept with its citations and shown as a claim, never as a fact.
6. **High-risk fields** (ARR, MRR, revenue, funding, valuation, customer count
   and concentration, employee count and size, investors, ownership, product
   sales) need a source other than the company itself to be `CORROBORATED`; a
   claim only the company makes is at most `UNSUPPORTED`.
7. **Staleness.** A `SUPPORTED` / `CORROBORATED` time-varying figure whose
   newest evidence (the year it states, else publication, else retrieval
   date) is older than `[assessment] stale_after_days` (730) is `STALE`; the
   value stays, dated.
8. `NOT_APPLICABLE` is never set automatically.

Each assessment stores its confidence components (best confidence, tier and
age per source) and, in `corroboration`, every observation's role:
`supports`, `qualifies` (a compatible bound), `contradicts` or `superseded`.
Rows written by `fa-v1` keep their old states as history.

List fields (investors, competitors, partners, features, use cases, prices,
business models, locations, …) are assessed per item. `number_of_products` is
the count of identified products, each cited by the observation naming it.
Every expected field that nothing supports is written as `UNKNOWN`.

## Dossier

`gtm_crawler.dossier.build_dossier` (schema 5, spec section 20) renders the
latest assessments: the
company (domain, subdomains from the crawl's own fetch records, and every
field), each product, and each competitor with its evidence basis and the
competitor-analysis fields of GTM Parameters V1.0. Every field has a `state`;
a `value` only where the state allows one; a `confidence`; and `citations`
(URL, exact quote, verbatim value, source tier and type, first-party flag,
retrieved and published times). `sections` groups the field names under
the spec's headings (identity, description and positioning, products, features,
pricing, target customers, industries and use cases, customer references,
traction, funding, partnerships and integrations, competitors, press, hiring,
geography, technology); `summary` counts field states; `freshness` gives the
last crawl, the oldest and newest evidence retrieval and the STALE fields;
`versions` are those the company's latest crawl recorded; `cost` totals the
job that ran that crawl (see Cost accounting). Served at
`GET /v1/companies/{id}/dossier`, JSON only: crawled text is data, never
rendered. Every API response carries `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
`Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'`,
`X-Frame-Options: DENY` and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`; the Swagger and
ReDoc HTML pages are off (`/openapi.json` remains). For local development,
`api.docs = true` serves Swagger UI at `/docs` (staging/prod refuse it);
`start.bat` with no argument starts the API and a worker with it on.

## Market research

Besides company dossiers, the service runs industry- and product-level
market research (`gtm_crawler/market/`): it plans searches for a research
request, fetches each result as a single page under the same safeguards
(network guard, robots.txt, throttle, raw store; links are never
followed), extracts quoted evidence and figures with rules (no LLM),
rates sources, keeps contradicting figures side by side, and returns a
structured report in which every finding points at its evidence. Product
research reuses a crawled company's dossier (`company_id`) and checks
proposed differentiators against competitors' own pages. Market sizing
(TAM/SAM/SOM, top-down and bottom-up, scenarios, sensitivity) and
opportunity scoring are deterministic calculations over explicit inputs.

Competitor ads: an operator records ads seen on Meta's Ad Library or Google's
Ads Transparency Center in a JSON file (no automated collection: their terms
and API coverage rule it out for India) and imports it; a product request
with `"include_ads": true` then reports what the competitors' ads say (known
competitors, and a `company_id`'s crawled competitors) and how their landing
pages match. `--ad-checklist` turns a crawl into the list of ad library
searches to do (docs/ad-intelligence-design.md).

```bash
gtm-market request.json --markdown       # one run in this process
gtm-market --ad-checklist 7 --markdown   # crawled company 7: which competitors' ads to capture, links
gtm-market --import-ads ads.json --operator "Asha" --screenshots shots/   # captured ads
curl -X POST localhost:8000/v1/market/research -H 'content-type: application/json'      -d '{"mode": "industry", "subject": "CRM software", "geography": "India"}'
```

`gtm-worker` runs queued research when no crawl job is waiting. Endpoints:
`POST /v1/market/research`, `GET /v1/market/research/{id}` (status,
progress), `.../report[?format=markdown]`, `.../cancel`, `.../ask` (the
evidence most relevant to a question; with a model configured, an answer
citing it), and `POST /v1/market/sizing`. Optional: OCR of page images
(`pip install -e ".[ocr]"`, `market.ocr_enabled`), text PDFs as evidence with
page-level, re-checkable quotes (`pip install -e ".[pdf]"`, `market.pdf_*`;
scanned PDFs are not read) and a model narrative
(`market.llm_model`). Methods, configuration and limitations:
docs/MARKET_ENGINE_IMPLEMENTATION.md; schemas: docs/MARKET_DATA_MODEL.md;
sizing: docs/MARKET_SIZING_METHODOLOGY.md; assessment:
docs/ARCHITECTURE_ASSESSMENT.md; post-implementation audit:
docs/POST_IMPLEMENTATION_AUDIT.md; live benchmark and quality gates:
docs/MARKET_BENCHMARK.md (`python -m gtm_crawler.market.benchmark`).

### Price extraction accuracy

Scored against hand-labelled prices (docs/MARKET_BENCHMARK.md):

| Set | Precision | Recall |
|---|---|---|
| Fixtures (360 labelled prices; development data, rules tuned on it) | 97.9% | 89.2% |
| Live pages (456 labelled prices, runs 6-7) | 95.7% | 73.7% |
| Held out (heldout-v2, 539 judgeable prices, never tuned on) | 82.7% | 54.2% |

The held-out row is the estimate for unseen sites. Amount and currency are
right on 95.9% of held-out matches; attributing a price to its plan and
product is the weakest part (13.4%). The fixture floor is enforced by
`test_pricing_fixture_benchmark_holds` (precision >= 97%, recall >= 88%).

## URL handling

`gtm_crawler.urls.normalize_url` produces one canonical form per resource:
lowercase scheme and host, IDNA hosts as punycode, default ports removed,
fragments dropped, dot segments resolved, duplicate slashes collapsed,
percent-escapes normalized, configured index files removed, and configured
tracking/session parameters stripped from the query and from `;path`
parameters, with remaining query parameters sorted. Non-http(s) schemes,
credentials in URLs and malformed hosts or ports are rejected, as are
non-canonical numeric IPv4 hosts (`http://2130706433/`, `http://0x7f.1/`,
`http://127.1/`, `http://192.168.001.1/`): resolvers disagree on them, so only
dotted-quad literals are accepted and judged by the network guard.

`gtm_crawler.frontier.Frontier` is the per-site priority queue. It admits a URL
only if it is new, on the target's registrable domain (subdomains included;
other sites are rejected as `external`), within the depth limit, and passes the
crawler-trap rules: URL length, path depth, query-parameter count, repeated
path segments, a cap per generalized URL shape (e.g. `/events/{n}/{n}`,
`/search?q`), a cap per host + first path segment, a cap per host
(`max_urls_per_host`), a cap on query-string variants per path (sort/filter
combinations), and session ids left in the URL. It also watches unique-content
yield: once `yield_min_fetches` pages of one URL shape or section were fetched,
the shape or section closes (`trap:low_yield`) if fewer than
`min_unique_yield` of them brought new content (duplicates, error pages); this
stops template duplication, pagination past the end and repeated redirects,
and already-queued URLs of a closed shape are dropped when claimed. Every
rejection is counted by reason. Limits are in the `[frontier]` config section.

## Fetching

`gtm_crawler.fetch.http.HttpFetcher` returns a `FetchResult` for every URL,
including failures (`blocked`, `timeout`, `too_large`, ...); it does not raise
for network, policy or HTTP errors.

- Redirects are followed manually (limit `fetch.max_redirects`); every hop is
  normalized and checked, and loops or non-http(s) targets are errors.
- 429, 500, 502, 503 and 504 responses and network errors are retried with
  jittered exponential backoff; `Retry-After` is honoured up to
  `fetch.max_retry_after`, beyond which the fetch gives up.
- Bodies are streamed; the transferred size and the decompressed size are each
  capped at `fetch.max_body_bytes`. Only `gzip` and `deflate` are accepted.
- `Set-Cookie` headers are never stored.
- `identity.user_agent_contact` must be set; it is sent in the User-Agent.

### Network guard

Every TCP connection goes through `gtm_crawler.netguard.NetGuard`: the host is
resolved once, every resolved address must be globally routable unicast
(private, loopback, link-local and cloud-metadata, CGNAT, documentation,
multicast and reserved ranges are refused, including IPv4 embedded in IPv6),
the port must be in `fetch.allowed_ports`, and the connection is made to the
checked address, so DNS rebinding cannot swap it afterwards. TLS still
verifies the certificate for the original hostname (verification cannot be
turned off). Because the check sits at the connection layer, it applies to
every redirect hop. Environment proxy settings are ignored.

- DNS answers are cached (`fetch.dns_cache_size` hosts for
  `fetch.dns_cache_seconds`); the policy is re-applied to cached answers on
  every connection.
- `fetch.allowed_networks` explicitly allows otherwise refused networks for a
  deployment (e.g. an internal test site); it is empty by default and logged
  when set. Cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254,
  100.100.100.200, 169.254.170.2) are refused regardless, also when embedded
  in IPv6 (IPv4-mapped, 6to4, NAT64, Teredo).
- IPv6 ranges Python's `is_global` wrongly reports as global are refused:
  `::/96` (IPv4-compatible), `::ffff:0:0:0/96`, `64:ff9b:1::/48` and
  `fec0::/10`.

### HTTP client

- **Timeouts:** connect (TCP and TLS, `connect_timeout`), time to first byte
  (`ttfb_timeout`: request sent to response headers received), each read or
  write (`read_timeout`, `write_timeout`), and the whole attempt
  (`attempt_timeout`).
- **Limits:** `max_body_bytes` on both transferred and decompressed size, and
  `max_decompression_ratio` (a body that expands more than 100x once past
  `decompression_ratio_min_bytes` is a decompression bomb).
- **Errors** are classified precisely (`FetchError`): policy (`blocked`,
  `invalid_redirect`, `too_many_redirects`, `redirect_loop`); network, retried
  with jittered backoff (`dns_failure`, `connect_timeout`, `ttfb_timeout`,
  `read_timeout`, `total_timeout`, `pool_timeout`, `connection_error`,
  `protocol_error`); and not retried (`tls_error`, `too_large`,
  `decompression_bomb`, `unsupported_content_type`, `unsupported_encoding`,
  `decode_error`).
- **Conditional requests** (`conditional_requests`, on): a URL fetched in an
  earlier crawl is requested with its `ETag` / `Last-Modified`; on `304 Not
  Modified` the stored, hash-verified body is reused (the fetch is recorded
  with status 304 and that body's hash). If the stored copy was pruned, the
  page is fetched whole.
- **HTTP/2** (`http2`, off; `pip install ".[http2]"`): negotiated by ALPN
  where the server offers it, through the same network guard.

### robots.txt and sitemaps

robots.txt is fetched once per origin and interpreted per RFC 9309 (via
Protego): a 4xx response means no restrictions (except 429), while 5xx or
network failure means nothing may be fetched. Rules from the group matching
`identity.product_token` take precedence over `*`. Rules are reused for at
most `robots.max_age_seconds` (24 hours, RFC 9309 section 2.4), then loaded
again. Every load is kept in `robots_snapshot` (the body in the raw store).

**Policy engine.** Every fetch is decided by `gtm_crawler/policy.py` as ALLOW,
BLOCK, DEFER or REJECT, and every non-ALLOW decision has a reason:
`out_of_scope` (REJECT), `robots_disallowed` or `robots_unreachable` (BLOCK).
Redirect hops are decided the same way before they are followed, so an
in-site redirect into a disallowed path is refused (`redirect_robots_disallowed`,
`redirect_off_site`). Each refusal sets the URL's state and reason and is
appended to `crawl_event` with the id of the robots snapshot that was
applied, so it can be audited from the exact rules; counts are in the crawl's
`stats.policy_decisions`. Frontier admission (traps: REJECT; depth and
breadth budgets: DEFER) is stored as the URL's state and reason.

Sitemaps are discovered from robots.txt declarations and `/sitemap.xml`,
following sitemap indexes (limits in `[sitemaps]`). XML, gzip and plain-text
sitemaps are supported. Only absolute URLs on the target site are kept, and
XML is parsed with entity resolution and network access disabled. Sitemap
requests obey robots.txt and the per-host throttle (including Crawl-delay),
and each is recorded in `sitemap_fetch`: status, error, URLs yielded, child
sitemaps, URLs dropped, the index that listed it, and the raw body's hash.
Sitemaps are discovery aids, not a complete picture of a site.

### Browser fallback

Pages are fetched over HTTP first. A page is rendered in headless Chromium
(Playwright) only when its parsed text is shorter than
`browser.min_text_chars` **and** it contains an executable `<script>` (JSON-LD
and JSON data blocks do not count), or when it has scripts and a `<noscript>`
notice saying its content needs JavaScript. The rendered DOM replaces the HTTP page
only if it yields more text; both attempts are recorded in `fetch`
(`method = http` / `browser`) and both bodies go to the raw store.

Chromium makes no network connections of its own. Every request it issues is
intercepted: the main document is served from the HTTP body already fetched;
only `document`, `script`, `xhr` and `fetch` requests are allowed (images,
styles, fonts and media are refused), and those are checked against
robots.txt, wait for the host's throttle slot and are performed by the HTTP
fetcher, so the network guard and size limits apply. As a backstop Chromium
runs behind a non-existent proxy (loopback included), WebSockets are mocked,
service workers are blocked and WebRTC cannot use non-proxied UDP.

One browser is launched per crawl, on first need; each render gets an isolated
context. Limits: `browser.concurrency` renders at a time, `max_renders` per
crawl, `render_timeout` per render and `settle_timeout` waiting for network
idle. Per render, the page may make at most `max_requests_per_render` requests
bringing in at most `max_bytes_per_render` bytes (further requests are refused
and counted; requests already in flight when the byte budget is crossed still
complete), and its final HTML may not exceed `fetch.max_body_bytes`.
Chromium runs with a capped V8 heap (`js_heap_mb`), is replaced after
`recycle_after_renders` renders (the old one closes when its last render
ends) and is relaunched after a crash, at most `max_restarts` times.
`circuit_failure_threshold` browser failures in a row stop rendering for
`circuit_cooldown_seconds` (pages are used as fetched). If Chromium cannot
start, the crawl continues over HTTP and the reason is reported in the crawl's
`stats.browser.unavailable`. `stats.browser` also reports the render cost
(`render_ms`, `requests`, `bytes`, `requests_refused`), `restarts` and the
`circuit` state; metrics `browser_render_duration_seconds` and
`browser_restarts_total`.

Chromium runs with its own OS sandbox (`browser.sandbox`, default on): each
renderer gets its own user, pid and network namespaces and a seccomp-bpf
filter. On Windows and macOS nothing extra is needed. On Linux it needs
unprivileged user namespaces; Docker's default seccomp profile forbids them,
so the worker runs with `deploy/chrome-seccomp.json` (Docker's default profile
plus `clone`/`unshare`; no added capabilities, not privileged), as
`docker-compose.yml` does. If the sandbox cannot start, Chromium does not
start and the crawl continues over HTTP (`stats.browser.unavailable`).
`staging`/`prod` refuse `sandbox = false` unless `allow_unsandboxed = true`.
The worker container has a measured 2.5 GiB memory limit; over it the kernel
kills a renderer first (that render fails), and a killed worker's job is
resumed by another worker when its lease expires.
`deploy/sandbox-test/run.sh` proves the sandbox, the absence of browser DNS
lookups and the memory-limit behaviour in the built image (test-only;
`GTM_SANDBOX_IMAGE=gtm-crawler pytest tests/test_hardening_phase18.py` runs it).

### External pages and search

The crawler never crawls the open web, and searches only where first-party
evidence is insufficient. After the company's site, its pages are extracted
and assessed; a company field still `UNKNOWN`, `UNSUPPORTED` (e.g. a revenue
figure only the company states), `STALE` or `CONFLICTING` is a gap. The bounded
external phase (`[external]`) then fetches single pages elsewhere for the
gaps only:

| Query | Runs when a gap is in |
|---|---|
| `"<company>" funding` | funding round, total funding, valuation, investors |
| `"<company>" revenue customers` | ARR, MRR, revenue, customer count / concentration |
| `"<company>"` on reference domains | profile facts: legal name, location, founding, size, ownership, funding |

- **Reference profiles** (LinkedIn, Crunchbase, G2, …) that the site links
  to, or that the profile query returns for this exact company (the URL must
  contain the company's name slug), up to `max_reference_pages`, when a
  profile fact is a gap.
- **Search results** of the queries above, up to `max_search_pages`,
  excluding the company's own site.
- **Competitor discovery**, every crawl (competitors are an open-ended
  list, so it is not gated by gaps), under its own budget:
  `max_competitor_queries` queries and `max_competitor_search_pages` pages.
  Queries come in families, taken in turn so a budget cut trims depth rather
  than whole families (`search.competitor_queries`):

  | Family | Queries |
  |---|---|
  | company name | `"<name>" competitors`, `alternatives`, `vs`, `versus`, `compared`, `comparison`, `switch from`, `migrate from`, `apps like`, `competitors <year>`, `review alternatives` |
  | other names the site uses | the first two of those |
  | each product name | `"<product>" alternatives`, `"<product>" vs` |
  | each product category / market domain | `best <c> software`, `top <c> tools`, `<c> companies`, `<c> vendors comparison` |
  | second round | for the `second_hop_competitors` strongest competitors known after round one's pages are extracted: `"<rival>" vs "<company>"`, `"<rival>" alternatives` |

  Competitor queries ask the provider to leave out the company's own domain.
  Their counts are in `stats.external.competitors`.

The gaps and queries are in the crawl's `stats.external`. If anything was
fetched, extraction and assessment run again over all pages.

Other sites' pages are read only when a link ties them to the company (to or
from its site, see identity resolution); the rest are stored and counted as
`identity_unverified_pages`. Many search results, list articles above all,
are kept and not read for this reason.

Each is fetched by our own fetcher (robots.txt, network guard, throttle, raw
store), recorded with `discovered_via` `link` or `search` (and the query), and
its links are recorded but never followed. Many profile sites disallow
crawlers in robots.txt; those pages are skipped and recorded as such.

Search uses [Exa](https://exa.ai/docs/reference/search) and only finds URLs;
evidence always comes from pages we fetched ourselves. Set the key with
`GTM__SEARCH__API_KEY`; without one, search is skipped. The key is never
stored in the crawl's configuration snapshot, statistics or query records.

Providers sit behind `search.SearchProvider` and return normalized results
(http(s) URLs only, each once, with their position). Transient failures
(network errors, 429, 5xx) are retried `search.max_retries` times with
jittered backoff, or after the provider's `Retry-After` when it is at most
`max_retry_after` seconds. `search.FailoverSearch` asks the configured
providers in order and falls over to the next when one fails; a provider
failing `circuit_failure_threshold` queries in a row is skipped for
`circuit_cooldown_seconds`. Only Exa is configured today; another provider is
one `SearchProvider` implementation in `make_search`. At most
`max_queries_per_crawl` gap queries are sent per crawl (competitor queries
have their own budget, above).

Every query is stored in `search_query` (template, query, domains, provider
that answered, `ok` / `failed` / `over_budget`, error, result count,
duration) and its results in `search_result` (position, URL, title,
published date). Results stay discovery: a result becomes evidence only
through the page we fetch for it.

Extractors treat other sites' pages as third-party: "we" / "our" there is the
publisher, so only the company's name refers to it; the page's own site name,
products, pricing and partner lists are not attributed to the company; and
JSON-LD counts only if it names the company's URL.

### Competitors

A company is a competitor only on explicit evidence, recorded as a
`competitor` observation and a `relationship` whose `basis` says which:

| Basis | Evidence |
|---|---|
| `comparison_page` | own page at `/vs/x`, `/compare/x`, `/a-vs-b` whose title names the other company ("Acme vs Globex"), matching the URL |
| `alternative_page` | own page at `/x-alternative(s)`, `/alternatives/x` titled "The best Globex alternative" |
| `stated_competition` | "we compete with …", "our competitors include …", or the same with the company's name on other sites |
| `stated_alternative` | "Acme is the leading alternative to …", "alternatives to Acme include …" |
| `stated_contrast` | a sentence opening "Unlike Globex, we …" (weaker) |
| `third_party_alternatives_list` | items under a page titled "Acme alternatives" / "Acme competitors" on another site |
| `third_party_comparison` | another site's page titled "Acme vs Globex" / "Compare Globex and Acme" |
| `listed_as_alternative` | another site's page titled "Globex alternatives" that lists Acme as one |

Co-mentions, customers, partners, integrations and investors are never
treated as competitors.

**Candidates.** On another site's list that has the company as one of its
items ("The 10 best CRM software", "Globex alternatives"), the other items are
recorded as `candidate_competitor` (relationship type and observation field,
basis `category_list`). Being listed together is a co-mention, so a candidate
is never a competitor: its domain is not resolved, its site is not crawled,
and the dossier lists it apart, under `candidate_competitors` (names also
found as competitors are left out there). The field is not one of the
company fields reported UNKNOWN when nothing was observed, so it never shows
as a gap.

A competitor's domain is taken only from a link on a page that named it: the
link's text is the competitor's name, or its domain's first label is the
name's slug ("Globex" -> `globex.com`), excluding the company's own site and
reference domains. If the evidence pages point to more than one candidate
domain, or none, the competitor stays unresolved. Each resolved competitor's
own site is then crawled (up to `max_competitor_crawls` competitors,
`competitor_max_pages` pages each, high-value pages first, no external phase)
and extracted, so competitor-analysis fields come from the competitor's own
first-party pages.

## Parsing

`gtm_crawler.parse.parse_page` turns an HTML body into a `ParsedPage`:

- **Text blocks**: each block-level element's visible text, with its XPath,
  tag, heading level and page region (`main`, `nav`, `header`, `footer`,
  `aside`). Regions come from semantic tags and ARIA roles, then from class
  and id hints for plain-`<div>` layouts (`site-menu`, `sidebar`,
  `cookie-banner`, `footer-links`, ...; whole tokens only). Navigation,
  headers and footers are kept and labelled rather than removed, since they
  often carry company facts. Script, style and similar non-content elements
  are skipped. `cleaned_text` joins all blocks; `main_text` only those outside
  boilerplate regions.
- **Links** with anchor text (falling back to image alt text), `rel`, and the
  surrounding block text; **lists**; **tables** as rows of cell text.
- **Metadata**: title, description (meta, OpenGraph or Twitter), headings,
  language, canonical URL, `<base>`, and all `<meta>` values (including
  OpenGraph and Twitter) keyed by name/property.
- **Schema.org microdata**: each `itemscope` with its types, its own
  `itemprop` values (nested items are separate) and its XPath.
- **JSON-LD**: every script kept as parsed; every typed node addressable by a
  JSON path (e.g. `$.jsonld[0]["@graph"][2]`). Malformed scripts are recorded
  in `json_ld_errors`.
- **Publication date** only from explicit markers (Article-type JSON-LD
  `datePublished`, `article:published_time` and similar meta tags,
  `<time itemprop="datePublished">`), with the source recorded in
  `published_at_source`. Dates mentioned in body text are not used.
- **Fingerprints**: `text_sha256` for exact duplicates, 64-bit `simhash` for
  near-duplicates, both over the page's content outside navigation, headers,
  footers and sidebars, so pages sharing a large menu are not mistaken for
  duplicates (a page with nothing else falls back to all its text).

Character encoding follows the WHATWG order: byte-order mark, HTTP charset,
`<meta>` charset, then UTF-8. Parsing never raises for bad input: pages are
untrusted, so any failure is reported in `parse_error`, the raw document is
still stored for replay, and the crawl continues. libxml2's safety limits
(nesting deeper than 256 elements, huge text nodes) drop content beyond them;
each limit hit is recorded in `parse_warnings`. Description, headings,
microdata, warnings and errors are stored in `page.meta`, and every page
records `parser_version`.

Main text uses the region heuristics above rather than a separate
main-content library (such as trafilatura): evidence quotes need the parser's
own blocks and XPaths, and one heuristic does not justify several more
dependencies. The replay corpus (Phase 15) is where its quality gets measured.

## Raw document store

The raw store keeps response bodies exactly as received, gzip-compressed and
named by the SHA-256 of the original bytes (`fetch.raw_sha256`). Reads verify
the hash. Two backends (`rawstore.backend`):

- `filesystem`: `<root>/<aa>/<bb>/<sha256>.gz`, written to a temporary file,
  fsynced and renamed.
- `s3`: any S3-compatible service (AWS S3, MinIO, R2), keys
  `<s3_prefix>sha256/<aa>/<bb>/<sha256>`. Credentials come from the standard
  AWS chain (environment, profile, instance role), never from the config.

A body is durable in the store before the database refers to it. Every
stored body has an `artifact` row (hash, size, content type, format version
`rawstore.ARTIFACT_VERSION`, first stored, pruned), written in the same
transaction as the fetch, robots.txt snapshot or sitemap request that cites
it; foreign keys keep the database from citing a document it has no record
of.

Retention (approved policy): documents that evidence cites and robots.txt
snapshots are kept forever; everything else is deleted by `gtm-crawl
--prune` once older than `rawstore.retention_days` (90). The artifact row
stays with `pruned_at` set, so lineage still shows what was retrieved; storing
the same body again clears it. Pruning also limits
what `--reextract` can re-read, so prune only crawls you will not re-extract.

## Replay and regression

`gtm_crawler/replay.py` (spec sections 24, 25, 29) re-parses every page of a
stored crawl from its raw artifact and runs the current extractors; nothing is
fetched. It compares the result with the crawl's current extraction run:

```bash
gtm-crawl --replay 42                    # dry run: computed in memory, nothing written
gtm-crawl --replay 42 --write            # new extraction run + re-assessment
gtm-crawl --replay-all [--organization 7] [--write] --fail-on-change   # CI gate
```

The JSON report lists observations `added`, `removed` and `changed` (with
per-field counts), old and new versions (`extraction.versions`), pages whose
artifact is missing or pruned (`unreadable_pages`; their observations show as
removed) and drafts the evidence checks refused (`rejected_drafts`). A write
replay also lists field-state transitions (e.g. `founded_year` `SUPPORTED` ->
`UNKNOWN`) between the latest assessment before and after. A dry run does not
assess (assessing needs recorded observations) and writes no rows, not even
products.

Observations are matched on page URL, field, product slug, qualifier,
verbatim and normalized value, evidence locator (type, locator, character
span) and related organization; a match whose kind, extractor, extraction
certainty or as-of date differs is `changed`. A bumped extractor version with
identical output is not a change; it shows in `versions.changed`.

Exit status: 2 for an unknown or unfinished crawl (`--replay`), 1 if any crawl
in a batch failed (the batch goes on and records the error), 1 with
`--fail-on-change` when any observation or field state changed, else 0.
`--reextract ID` stays as the write path that prints the dossier instead of
the comparison.

Every extraction run records `trigger` (`crawl` or `replay`) and `versions`
(`versions.current_versions`; NULL for runs made before migration 0014, i.e.
unknown). A write replay supersedes the earlier run without deleting it, so
lineage (`GET /v1/companies/{id}/lineage/{field}`) and the dossier trace to the
new observations while the old ones stay queryable by `extraction_id`.

## Crawl behaviour

A crawl covers the seed's registrable domain including subdomains.

1. robots.txt is loaded; if it is unreachable, nothing else is fetched and the
   crawl is `failed`.
2. Sitemaps seed the frontier; the seed URL is always fetched first.
3. Up to `crawl.concurrency` URLs are processed at a time, claimed from
   `crawl_url` by priority under a lease. Each is checked against robots.txt,
   waits for its host's slot (`per_host_concurrency`, and a gap of at least
   `min_delay_seconds` or the site's `Crawl-delay`, whichever is larger), then
   is fetched. Redirects that leave the site are not followed. Timeouts,
   network errors, 429 and 5xx go to `retry_wait` and are tried again after
   `retry_delay_seconds` (or the server's `Retry-After`, if longer), up to
   `max_url_attempts`; a 429 or 503 also holds back every request to that
   host. Security rejections are `blocked` and never retried.
   Rate control adapts per host (`gtm_crawler/throttle.py`): 429/503 halve
   the host's concurrency and add an extra delay, timeouts and 5xx halve it,
   slow responses (`slow_response_seconds`) lower it by one, and a run of
   `rate_recover_after` fast responses raises it back. Crawl-delay is never
   shortened; a Crawl-delay above `robots.max_crawl_delay_seconds` defers the
   origin (`crawl_delay_too_long`) instead. After
   `circuit_failure_threshold` failures in a row a host's circuit opens
   (`gtm_crawler/breaker.py`): its URLs go back to `retry` for the cooldown
   without using an attempt, then one probe decides whether it closes. Crawl
   stats `rate_control` and `circuits` name the hosts affected.
4. HTML responses are stored in the raw store, parsed (rendered in the
   browser first if the HTML has too little text; see above), and saved as a
   `page`.
   A page whose text exactly matches, or nearly matches
   (`near_duplicate_bits`), an earlier page is saved with
   `duplicate_of_page_id` and its links are not followed.
5. All links are saved. In-site links enter the frontier; external links are
   recorded only, and profiles on `reference_domains` are flagged as
   references. A same-site `rel=canonical` target is queued as well, and so
   are URLs named by the page's JSON-LD (`url`, `@id`, `mainEntityOfPage`,
   `sameAs`; recorded as links with rel `structured-data`). URLs the frontier
   refuses are stored with the reason, up to `frontier.max_recorded_rejections`
   per crawl: crawler traps as `trap`, depth and breadth budgets as
   `deferred` (a deferred URL found again within budget becomes `eligible`).
6. The crawl ends `completed` when the frontier is empty, `partial` when
   `max_pages`, `max_bytes`, `max_duration_seconds` or `max_error_rate` stops
   it first, `paused` when asked to stop (resumable), and `failed` when no
   page could be fetched. Every URL's progress is kept in `crawl_url`:
   `eligible` -> `leased` -> `fetching` -> `fetched` -> `parsed` ->
   `processed` (duplicates too, with reason `duplicate`), or `blocked`
   (robots.txt, network guard, off-site redirect; with the reason), `retry`
   (retried later) or `failed`. A dead worker's `leased` to `parsed` URLs are
   claimed again when their lease expires. Every attempt is kept in `fetch`,
   with its duration (and, for browser renders, the
   reason the render was triggered). Pages record the parser version.

### Prioritization

Each URL's score is the sum of named signals: GTM terms in its path
(`high_value_terms` such as pricing, products, customers, about, press;
`medium_value_terms` such as docs, blog, careers), the same terms in its link
text and surrounding text, the topic of the page linking to it (its title
and meta description), its sitemap priority, its freshness (sitemap
`lastmod`, full credit today, none after `freshness_horizon_days`), its depth,
and how many pages link to it; `low_value_terms` (tag, login, cart, ...)
lower it. The breakdown is stored with the URL (`crawl_url.priority_signals`,
always summing to `score`), so the crawl order can be explained. Terms only
order the crawl; they never exclude an in-site URL. Weights and term lists
are in `[priority]`.

## Logging

Logs are JSON, one object per line, with an event name and structured fields:

```json
{"ts": "...", "level": "INFO", "logger": "gtm_crawler.fetch", "event": "fetch.completed", "job_id": 7, "crawl_id": 12, "status": 200}
```

Every record logged while a job or crawl runs carries its `job_id` and
`crawl_id` (correlation ids), including records from worker threads.
Untrusted text cannot split a record (control characters, NEL, U+2028 and
U+2029 are escaped), and configured secrets, URL user info
(`https://***@host`, `https://***:***@host`) and bearer tokens are redacted.

### Tracing

Work is traced on the stdlib (`gtm_crawler/tracing.py`), with W3C Trace
Context ids, and emitted through the same JSON log; no collector is needed.
Each unit of work logs one `span` record when it ends: `http.request` (API,
`/v1` only), `job.run`, `crawl.site`, `url.process`, `url.external`,
`fetch.http`, `fetch.browser`, `parse`, `store`, `search.query`, `extract`,
`assess`, `dossier.build`. A span record has `span`, `trace_id`, `span_id`,
`parent_span_id`, `start`, `duration_ms`, `status` (`ok`/`error`), `error`
(the exception class only) and the correlation fields `job_id`, `crawl_id`,
`url`, `domain`, `worker`, `stage`; other attributes (HTTP status, provider,
outcome) are under `attrs`. Every log line inside a span carries its
`trace_id` and `span_id`. Page content is never put in a span, and span
records are redacted like any log line.

`POST /v1/crawls` continues an incoming `traceparent` header (an invalid one
starts a new trace) and every `/v1` response returns one. A new job keeps its
request's trace (in the `created` event of `crawl_event`) and the worker
continues it, so the API request, the job, its crawls and every URL share one
`trace_id`; asking for an existing job returns that job's `traceparent`.
To follow one job:

```sh
jq -c 'select(.trace_id == "4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736" and .event == "span")
       | {span, duration_ms, status, url, parent_span_id}' worker.log
```

`traceparent` is never sent to crawled sites: it would let third parties link
our requests together and would leak internal ids. `[logging]
trace_sample_rate` (default 1.0) is the share of new traces whose span records
are logged; ids are always assigned and logged.

## Metrics

Prometheus metrics are served by the worker (`worker.metrics_host` /
`metrics_port`, 127.0.0.1:9100 by default) and the API (`/metrics`):
`crawl_jobs_total{status}`, `crawl_duration_seconds`, `urls_discovered_total`,
`urls_fetched_total`, `http_requests_total{outcome}`,
`http_request_duration_seconds`, `http_retries_total{kind}`,
`blocked_requests_total{reason}`, `bytes_downloaded_total`,
`browser_fallback_total{trigger,outcome}`, `pages_parsed_total`,
`evidence_created_total`, `observations_created_total`, `field_unknown_total`,
`field_conflict_total`, `frontier_depth`, `extraction_duration_seconds` and
`db_query_duration_seconds`; cost accounting adds `cost_usd_total{kind}` and
`resource_usage_total{kind,unit}`.

## Cost accounting

Spec section 27 (`gtm_crawler/cost.py`, table `cost_event`). When a job run
ends, each of the job's site crawls (competitors included) is measured from
what it recorded, and this run's database statement time is added:

| kind | units | measured from |
|---|---|---|
| `http` | `requests`, `bytes` | fetch, robots.txt and sitemap rows; response body bytes |
| `browser` | `renders`, `seconds`, `bytes` | browser fetch rows; render time and bytes from crawl stats |
| `search` | `queries` (per provider) | answered `search_query` rows |
| `storage` | `bytes` | raw artifacts the crawl references (uncompressed size) |
| `worker` | `seconds` | crawl wall time (resumed crawls included) |
| `database` | `seconds` | statement time of the job run (per run) |

Each row is priced with `[cost]` (USD per unit; bytes per GB). **A price of 0
(the default) means unknown: the quantity is recorded and its cost is 0.**
Rows are keyed per crawl measurement and per run, so recording again after a
retry, resume or lost lease updates rows instead of duplicating them. Limits:
a run that dies before it records loses its database time; an artifact
shared by several crawls counts for each; robots.txt body bytes are not
counted. The job result (`GET /v1/crawls/{id}`), the dossier's `cost` block
and `GET /v1/companies/{id}/cost` report the totals.

Crawl quality (spec section 27; `quality.py`, observed once per job that
finishes COMPLETED or FAILED): `crawl_urls_total{outcome}` (discovered,
eligible, fetched, blocked, deferred, trapped), `pages_relevant_total`,
`external_sources_total`, `crawl_duplicate_ratio`,
`crawl_unique_content_yield`, `crawl_gaps_unanswered`; per assessment run
`field_states_total{state}`; `search_requests_total{provider,outcome}`.
For alerts: `worker_last_seen_timestamp_seconds`, `job_leases_expired_total`
(a worker died or hung), `circuit_opened_total{breaker}` (host, search,
browser), `database_up` (the scrape's queue queries answered),
`browser_processes_resident_memory_bytes` (the worker's child processes,
i.e. Playwright's driver and Chromium; Linux `/proc` only), plus the queue
gauges `queue_jobs{state}`, `queue_urls{state}`, `dead_letter_open{entity}`.

`deploy/prometheus/alerts.yml` alerts on queue starvation, stale or crashing
workers, abandoned jobs, HTTP error spikes, dead-letter growth, slow or
unreachable PostgreSQL, browser memory and crashes, search and browser
outages, and targets down (scrape jobs `gtm-worker` and `gtm-api`).
`deploy/grafana/crawl-health.json` is a dashboard of crawl health and
quality (import it and pick the Prometheus data source). Every metric they
use is checked against the code by `tests/test_quality_phase14.py`;
thresholds are starting points.

`GET /v1/crawls/{id}/quality` returns one job's quality report, computed
from the database; the job's `result.quality` keeps it as it was when the
job stopped. It has URL counts by outcome, HTTP errors by class, parsed,
duplicate, unique and relevant pages (relevant: cited as evidence by the
latest extraction), evidence and observations, the company's field states,
external source domains, browser renders, search queries, bytes, fetch
latency p50/p90/p99 by method (nearest rank over `fetch.duration_ms`),
duplicate ratio, unique-content yield (unique pages / HTTP fetches) and
completeness (frontier left, stop reasons, budget exhaustion, fields still
open). `report_version` (also in the job's `versions`) changes whenever a
definition does.

## Testing

```bash
pytest                 # all tests; database tests need `docker compose up -d`
pytest -m "not db"     # skip database tests
pytest -m security     # adversarial security tests (docs/security-review.md)
ruff check . && ruff format --check . && mypy
```

Database tests run against a separate `gtm_crawler_test` database (override
with `GTM_TEST_DATABASE_URL`). The S3 backend is tested against an in-memory
fake; set `GTM_TEST_S3_ENDPOINT` (e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:8333` from the
compose `s3` service, with any `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`)
to also test a real endpoint. They are skipped, and reported as skipped, if
PostgreSQL is unreachable. Browser tests need `playwright install chromium`.

No test touches the real Internet: sites, search, robots.txt and the
optional model endpoint are simulated with `httpx.MockTransport`
(market research: `tests/test_market.py`, offline; `tests/test_market_service.py`,
PostgreSQL), and the browser tests route Chromium through the
same mock (or through the real network guard, to prove it cannot reach
private addresses).

`tests/test_truthfulness.py` runs the whole pipeline against simulated sites
and checks the dossier a user would read: a site without revenue yields
`UNKNOWN` (even when a revenue page exists behind robots.txt); "we raised
$10M" gives a funding round but `UNKNOWN` valuation and total funding;
"more than 500 customers" is never 500 exactly; employee count is not turned
into company size; prices are not turned into revenue; facts about other
companies on the blog are not attributed; duplicates are not extra sources;
an empty site is `UNKNOWN` throughout; a JavaScript site is rendered and
cited; disagreeing sources are `CONFLICTING` with both citations; and every
stored quote is re-verified against its raw document.

### Artifact corpus

`tests/corpus/<case>/` holds synthetic pages exactly as the raw store keeps
them, so parser and extractor changes are regression-tested without
re-crawling (spec sections 24, 29). Each case has `artifact.html` (the
response body, byte for byte), `meta.json` (`url`, `content_type` header,
`subject_domain`, `first_party`, `description`; optional `company_names`
learned from the company's other pages, and `known_issue` for a truthfulness
bug the golden currently records) and `expected.json`, the golden: parse
facts plus every observation (value, normalized value, qualifier, product,
relationship, quote and locator, certainty), sorted, with the parser and
extractor versions that produced it. Every observation must pass
`check_draft`, or the case fails.

```bash
python -m gtm_crawler.corpus                 # compare; prints a diff, exits 1 on mismatch
python -m gtm_crawler.corpus --case NAME     # one case
python -m gtm_crawler.corpus --update        # rewrite the goldens (also writes new cases')
```

To add a case, create the directory with `artifact.html` and `meta.json`,
run `--update --case NAME`, and read `expected.json` critically before
committing it. Version rule: output that changes must come with a version
bump. If a golden differs while the responsible version is unchanged
(`PARSER_VERSION` for parse facts; the extractor's `VERSION`, or the parser's,
for its observations) the corpus fails with "bump it"; after bumping, review
the diff and run `--update`. `tests/test_corpus_phase15.py` runs the corpus
under pytest.

A figure a sentence attributes to a third party ("LinkedIn lists Acme in the
51-200 employees band", "according to Crunchbase, ...") keeps that attribution
in its normalized value: `status: "reported"`, `reported_by: "LinkedIn"` (company
and traction extractors; a company citing itself or its executives is not a
report). Assessment needs no change for it: the claim's source is the page it
was read from, so LinkedIn quoted on the company's site is a company-site claim,
never an independent source.

### Load testing

`python -m gtm_crawler.loadtest <scenario|all> [--scale N] [--out results.json]`
runs the real worker, pipeline and job API against a simulated web (spec
sections 29, 32, 35): generated sites behind `httpx.MockTransport` with
latency, jitter and injected 500s, a timed stand-in for Chromium, and a
scripted search provider that can fail with 5xx, timeouts and 429. Scenarios:
`deep_single_domain`, `many_domain`, `browser_heavy`, `provider_degradation`,
`db_pressure`, `queue_pressure`, `crash_recovery`. Each prints PASS/FAIL per
acceptance check and exits 1 on any failure; `--out` writes wall time,
pages/s, fetch latency percentiles, peak memory, DB statement counts, queue
depths and warnings as JSON. The scratch database (`GTM_LOAD_DATABASE_URL` or
`--database-url`, default `gtm_crawler_load_scratch`) is dropped and recreated;
names without "load" are refused. `tests/test_load_phase16.py` runs every
scenario at small scale; `GTM_LOAD=1 pytest -m load` runs them at full scale.
Measured results and what the simulation does not capture:
[docs/load-results.md](docs/load-results.md).

## Backup, restore and verification

`python -m gtm_crawler.backup backup --out DIR` dumps PostgreSQL (`pg_dump
-Fc`) and then copies the filesystem raw store (database first: bodies are
stored before rows cite them), with a `manifest.json` (snapshot time, alembic
revision, versions, dump SHA-256, raw object count). `python -m
gtm_crawler.backup restore DIR` restores into an empty database, copies the raw
store back, runs `alembic upgrade head` and verifies. `gtm-crawl --verify` is a
read-only integrity check: every recorded raw document is present with a
matching SHA-256, and every non-UNKNOWN field traces to observations, evidence,
a page and its raw artifact; exit 1 on any problem. `pg_dump`/`pg_restore` run
behind `GTM_PG_COMMAND_PREFIX` (e.g. `docker exec -i -e PGPASSWORD
scraper10-postgres-1`). `python -m gtm_crawler.drill` is the automated restore
drill (crawl, kill a worker mid-crawl, back up, destroy, restore, check the
dossier, replay and resumption). RPO/RTO, measured times and the runbook:
[docs/disaster-recovery.md](docs/disaster-recovery.md).
